#!/bin/sh
#
# EzPlate pre-push gate. Four checks, all deterministic, all a few seconds.
#
#   1. npm test          — the suite must be green.
#   2. npm run smoke     — the jsdom walkthrough, which is NOT part of `npm test`.
#   3. mutate --changed  — every test file or target function this branch touched must still KILL
#                          the mutants of the code it claims to pin.
#   4. review artifact   — a diff that changes WHAT RUNS must be accompanied by a committed review
#                          under docs/reviews/. See WHY (4) below.
#
# WHY (2) IS HERE, ADDED IN 192. It was not, and CI runs it as a separate job — so a batch could
# have `npm test` green, the mutation gate green and Playwright green locally, and still go red on
# push. That happened twice: 174 and 192, both times on the SAME assertion, which is stated in three
# places of which `tests/smoke.js` is the only one outside `npm test`. 174 left a warning in that
# file saying exactly this; it did not help, because it is only readable by someone already opening
# the file they are about to break.
# The gate is the mechanism and the comment was not. Eight seconds is worth it.
#
# WHY (3) EXISTS. A test that cannot fail is this project's most common defect class: ten instances
# across batches 165-176, several written INSIDE the file that warns about the pattern. A rule in a
# context file is advice a model can reason past; this is not. `tests/mutation/targets.js` says which
# functions are covered and which test files claim them.
#
# It is NOT a substitute for the `code-review` agent, which is mandatory and reads for the things no
# mutant can see: a comment whose stated mechanism is backwards, a premise that is wrong, a control
# that does nothing.
#
# WHY (4) EXISTS, ADDED IN 207 (QUEUE item 0d). That agent is the most productive gate in this
# process and it was the only one leaving NO TRACE — not on the PR, not in CI, not in git, not in
# the handover template. Six batches that shipped a client asset to production have no record of it,
# and only one of those is knowable because its brief said to skip it. The other five are silence,
# and silence is indistinguishable from compliance. The 13 Aug "NOT SKIPPABLE BY INSTRUCTION" rule
# fixed the one visible case and could do nothing about the five that were not, because it is
# another convention layered on the convention that failed.
# The decision lives in tests/review/check.js rather than in this script, and is unit-tested by
# tests/review-gate.test.js — a rule that lives in a hook can only be tested by pushing, so it never
# is, which is the same shape as the defect it fixes.
# ⚠️ It is a gate against FORGETTING, not against dishonesty: it cannot tell whether a review
# happened, only whether a file says one did. The handover's mandatory `## Review` section is the
# half a human reads.
#
# INSTALL (once per clone — hooks are not carried by git):
#   git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# ESCAPE HATCH: `git push --no-verify`. If you use it, say so in the handover — an unexplained
# skip is the same silence this gate replaced.

set -e

echo "pre-push 1/4: npm test"
npm test --silent

echo "pre-push 2/4: npm run smoke (jsdom — not covered by npm test)"
npm run smoke --silent

echo "pre-push 3/4: mutation gate (changed scope)"
node tests/mutation/run.js --changed

echo "pre-push 4/4: review artifact"
node tests/review/check.js
